      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.612,
            0.069,
            0.815,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": "The World of Numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.856,
            0.068,
            0.883,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": "59"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.11,
            0.603,
            0.144
          ],
          "content": "**Example 4:** Convert 0.35 into the form $\\frac { p } { q }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.144,
            0.477,
            0.179
          ],
          "content": "We know that $0.35 = \\frac{35}{100} = \\frac{7}{20}.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.182,
            0.885,
            0.237
          ],
          "content": "Now, let us understand how to convert non-terminating repeating decimals into the form $\\frac { p } { q }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.247,
            0.522,
            0.267
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Case 2: Pure repeating decimals"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.267,
            0.886,
            0.308
          ],
          "content": "A pure repeating decimal is one in which a digit or a sequence of digits begins repeating immediately after the decimal point."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.31,
            0.59,
            0.343
          ],
          "content": "**Example 5:** Convert $0 . \\overline { { 6 } }$ into the form $\\frac { p } { q }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.343,
            0.349,
            0.364
          ],
          "content": "**Step 1:** Let $x = 0 . \\overline { { 6 } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.369,
            0.885,
            0.418
          ],
          "content": "**Step 2:** Since one-digit repeats, multiply both sides by $10^{1} = 10$ , we get $1 0 x = 6 . \\overline { { 6 } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.424,
            0.709,
            0.446
          ],
          "content": "**Step 3:** Subtract the first equation from the second:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.45,
            0.381,
            0.471
          ],
          "content": "10x - x = 6.\\overline{6} - 0.\\overline{6} = 6.",
          "image_source": "images/page_18_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.485,
            0.595,
            0.527
          ],
          "content": "**Step 4:** Solve for $x : 9x = 6$ Thus, $\\frac{6}{9}=\\frac{2}{3}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.546,
            0.735,
            0.58
          ],
          "content": "**Example 6:** Convert 0.45 into the form $\\frac{p}{q}.$ Let $x = 0 . { \\overline { { 4 5 } } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.585,
            0.883,
            0.627
          ],
          "content": "Since two digits repeat, multiply by $1 0 ^ { 2 } = 1 0 0$ . We get $100x = 45.\\overline{45}$ Subtracting the two equations, we get $9 9 x = 4 5$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.639,
            0.317,
            0.673
          ],
          "content": "So, $x = { \\frac { 4 5 } { 9 9 } } = { \\frac { 5 } { 1 1 } } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.699,
            0.559,
            0.72
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Case 3: General repeating decimals"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.724,
            0.884,
            0.765
          ],
          "content": "A general repeating decimal has some non-repeating digits just after the decimal point, followed by a repeating block."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.771,
            0.601,
            0.803
          ],
          "content": "**Example 7:** Convert 0.16 into the form $\\frac { p } { q }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.804,
            0.367,
            0.826
          ],
          "content": "**Step 1:** Let $x = 0 . 1 { \\overline { { 6 } } } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.833,
            0.886,
            0.874
          ],
          "content": "**Step 2:** First, shift the decimal to place the repeating part immediately after the decimal point."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
